<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Bithynia</h1><p class="st-meta">297 BCE – 74 BCE · capital Nicomedia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Bithynia, a Hellenistic kingdom of north-western Anatolia that invited the Galatians in, sheltered Hannibal and was bequeathed to Rome.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Bithynians, a people of Thracian origin, lived south of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a>. Their dynast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zipoetes-i-of-bithynia">Zipoetes I</a> resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, and in 297 BCE he took the title king, founding a dynasty that lasted over two centuries.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedes-i-of-bithynia">Nicomedes I</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a> as his capital in 264 BCE and invited Celtic warriors into Asia, who settled in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/galatia">Galatia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prusias-i-of-bithynia">Prusias I</a> expanded the kingdom, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Prusa</a> and sheltered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>, who took poison at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyssa">Libyssa</a> in 183 BCE rather than be handed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The kings promoted Greek city life.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedes-iv">Nicomedes IV</a>, restored to his throne by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> in his will in 74 BCE. The bequest provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-mithridatic-war">Third Mithridatic War</a> with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus</a>, and Bithynia became a Roman province.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-bithynia.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Bithynia" width="320" height="145"><figcaption>Image: Ennomus · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bithynian_Kingdom.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>297 BCE – 74 BCE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nicomedia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nicomedes I, Prusias I, Nicomedes IV</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek and Thracian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek, Bithynian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bithynia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-186&amp;focus=kingdom-of-bithynia" aria-label="Kingdom of Bithynia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5441 730 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Bithynia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5761 986L5548 987M5761 986L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5548 987h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5761 986h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5761 986h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-186&amp;focus=kingdom-of-bithynia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
